# Asking for help by voice, the demo beat, against the FAKE cloud (test/EXPECTED.md, GW1). The "caretaker" is the
# fake cloud's help keys: a made-up Maya, no real phone. The fake cloud understands no speech: `key +` makes its
# next turn answer as the server does when the model chooses ask_for_help.
# Run: node dist/walker.mjs simulate --screen --script test/sim/help-voice.walk

# 1. Boot; the first help poll answers 200, so Talk turns carry can_ask_for_help.
expect spoke Sophi is on.
expect link online
expect heartbeat ok
wait 3000

# 2. "I've fallen. Please get Maya." The reply, then the countdown from 8.
key +
key t
expect mic open
wait 1200
key t
expect spoke I'll ask Maya for help in 8 seconds. Press any button to stop.
expect help help_countdown 8
expect screen help_countdown
expect help help_countdown 1

# 3. At zero: asked, and sent to Maya's phone.
expect 12000 spoke Asking for help.
expect spoke Sent to Maya's phone.
expect help help open
expect screen help

# 4. Maya's (fake) app shows it, then she taps "I'm on my way".
key help-shown
expect 8000 spoke It's showing on Maya's phone.
key help-coming
expect 8000 spoke Maya is on the way.
expect help help open shown coming

# 5. Confirm twice: she's okay. Then the clock again.
key c
expect spoke Press Confirm again to tell Maya you're okay.
expect help help open shown coming confirm_okay
key c
expect spoke Okay. Maya will see that you're okay.
expect help help closed okay
expect 15000 help none
expect screen ambient
